Welsh additional MPs are worried that they look like puppets.
Some MPs don't understand why their parliamentary status should change the status of a "member of the Welsh Parliament".
Deputies were indignant because of the plans to change the name of the local assembly to the name "Parliament of Wales".
The MPs of all political views believe that this is some kind of abuse.
One leiborian deputy said that his colleagues were concerned that it rhymes with the words "stupidly" and "indirectly".
For readers outside Wales: In Welsh twp means "stupid", and pwp means "surprise".
The deputy said that her group was completely unsatisfied and proposed alternatives.
Welsh conservators said that their group was ready to discuss the change of the meeting name, but noted that it was a short verbal transition from a member of the Welsh parliament to "puppets".
In this consequence, the Welsh letter w is pronunciated in the same way as the Yorkshire English pronunciation.
The Commission of the Assembly, which is now developing a draft law on amending the name, made a statement: "The final decision on any description of what deputies of the assembly is called will, of course, be a question of the members".
The Law on the Government of Wales from 2017 gives the Welsh Assembly the right to change its own name.
In June, the Commission published the results of the national consultation on proposals, which showed broad support to call the Assembly of the Parliament of Wales.
With regard to the title of additional deputy, the Commission favored the Members of the Weldeland or CNC Parliament, but the CUP option received the greatest support during public consultations.
Additional MPs obviously offer alternative options, but a way to achieve consensus can be a headache for the chairman, Eline Jones, who is supposed to submit a bill on changes for a few weeks.
Legislations on reforms will include other changes in the work of the Assembly, for example the rules on the disqualification of additional deputies and changes to the structure of the committee system.
Additional MPs will hold a final vote on the question of how they should be called if they are doing their legislative work.
Macedonians vote in a referendum on changing the country's name
On Sunday, voters will vote for changing their country’’’s “Republic of North Macedonia” or against it.
The national vote was organized in order to resolve a multi-annual issue with neighboring Greece, which has its own an area called Macedonia.
In Athens, they have long insisted that the name of their northern neighbor represents a claim to their territory, which prevented the inclusion and friendship of Macedonia in the EC and the North Atlantic Alliance.
Macedonia's President George Ivanov, an adversary of the referendum to change its name, said that he would not take part in the vote.
But supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, argue that changing the name is just a price to be paid for joining the EC and the North Atlantic Alliance.
