Shark was paralyzed a 13-year-old boy while diving to catch lobusters in California.
On Saturday, the shark was attacked and invaded a 13-year-old boy who hunted lobusters in California on the opening day of the lobster fishing season.
The attack took place around 7 a.m. near Becon-Beach's Beach in Ensinitas.
Chad Hamel told KSWB-TV in San Diego that on Saturday morning he practiced diving with his friends for half an ix half an hour when he heard the boy screaming, begging for help with the rest to help pull him out of the water.
At first, Hamel assumed that it was just an excitement from catching lobusters, but then he "understood that something bad had happened: "I was bitten!
I've been bitten!"
“His entire clutch was torn,” Hamel said, adding that he noticed this when he approached the boy.”
I shouted to everyone to come out of the water, "There's a shart in the water!" added Hamel.
The boy was transported by air to Raidy's children's hospital in San Diego, where he is in a critical state.
The type of shark that caused the attack is unknown.
Rescuer Captain Larry Jailes said at a media outburst briefing that the shark had been spotted a few weeks earlier, but it was decided that it was a safe variety.
Jailes added that the victim suffered an injury to the top of the torso.
Officials have closed their approaches to the beach from Ponta Beach in Casabodlada to Swami in Essinitas for 48 hours to conduct an investigation and for security purposes.
Jailes noted that more than 135 shark species live in this area, but most of them are considered safe.
Sainsbury's plans to advance to the British beauty market
Sainsbury’s is going to work with Boots, Superdrug and Debhams, discovering departments with beauty products in the department stores where trained professionals will work.
As part of a significant promotion to the British beauty market with a turnover of €2.8 billion, which continues to grow, while the volume of sales of fashionable clothes and households are decreasing, larger beauty houses will be presented in 11 salons across the country and also in other new salons next year, which demonstrates success.
Investing in the sphere of beauty comes because supermarkets are looking for a way to fill a seat on the shells that were previously occupied by TVs, microwave ovens and household goods.
Sainsbury’s stated that it would double the volume of beauty products by offering up to 3000 products, including brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and PawPaw, which will appear for the first time.
Existing range from L'Oreal, Maybelline and Burt’s Bees will also be given to more space in branded departments similar to those that can be found in stores such as Boots.
Large stores are also releassing their Boutiqué cosmetic line, so that most products will be suitable for vegans, and this trend is becoming more in demand among young buyers.
In addition, the Fragrance Store will test a concession in two Sainsbury's stores, the first of which opened last week in Kroydon in southern London, and the second one will open in Selly-Owk (Birmingham) at the end of this year.
Shopping on the Internet and the transition to buying a small number of products daily in local stores near the home leads to the fact that supermarkets have to make more efforts to be more attractive to visitors.
Mike Coupé, Executive Director of Sainsbury's, said retail outlets would be more like department stores, a supermarket chain is trying to compete with Aldi, Lid's disprovers with more services and non-food products.
Sainsbury's has placed Argos outlets in hundreds of stores, and has also housed a few Habitats points since two years ago acquired both networks, which he said contributed to the growth of food sales and made the acquisition more lucrative.
The previous attempt of supermarkets to reconstruct their beauty and pharmacies did not end successfully.
Sainsbury's tested a joint venture from Boots in the early 2000's, but an attempt to divide revenue from pharmacy stores expired after a series of disassemblies in supermarkets.
The new strategy came afrored three years ago Sainsbury's selling its pharmacy business along with 281 Celesio store to the Lloyds Pharmacy Pharmacy pharmacy for £125 million.
It has been stated that Lloyds played a role in this regard by extending the assortment of luxury skin care brands, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy in four stores.
Paul Mills-Hicks, the commercial director of Sainsbury's, said: "We have changed the appearance of our beauty salons to improve the conditions for our customers.
We have also invested in the training of our employees who will be able to offer their recommendations.
Our range of brands is designed taking into account any needs, and an attractive environment and comfortable accommodation make us the best beauty home, which confronts the old way of shopping."
