Six people were arrested in Barcelona on Saturday after supporters of independence collided with the police for the protection of public order, and thousands of others joined the demonstration in marking the first anniversary of a polarization vote in Catalony.
A group of separatists in masks, who were restrained by the police for the protection of public order, throwing them with eggs and blossomed with powder paint, creating dark clouds of dust on the streets, which are usually filled with tourists.
Later on the same day, the fight broke out when the police used their truncheons to curb demonstrators.
Within a few hours, a group of fighters for independence who chanted "Don't Forget, don't forgive", faced with present trade union figures who shouted: "Long live Spain".
According to the local press press, fourteen people have been treated from minor injuries sustained during the protests.
Tension in the region seeking independence remains high a year after the October 1 referendum, which Madrid found to beillegane, but noted by Catalan separatists.
Voters in the vast majority preferred independence, although the voter turnout was low, which was largely explained by a boycott of voting.
According to Catalan authorities, last year almost 1,000 people were injured after the police tried to stop voting at polling stations throughout the region as a result of the actions of angry demonstrators.
Groups in favor of independence spent Friday night to prevent demonstrations in support of Spain's national police.
Demonstrations continued, but the protesters were forced to go the other way.
68-year-on-year-old Narsis Thermes, an electrician who is present in a separatist protest with his his wife, said that he no longer hopes for the independence of Catalonia.
"Last twisting one of the best moments.
I've seen my parents weeping with joy when they could vote, but now we're tired," he said.
In spite of a vital but insignificant victory in the regional elections in December last year, the Catalan parties in favor of independence have tried to maintain momentum this year with many of their most famous leaders and turned out to be either Voluntary Exiled or in prison, waiting for the trial for organizing a referendum and the subsequent declaration of independence.
The 42-year-old mechanic Joán Puch, who is recording a protest in support of the police on the phone, said that the conflict was provoked by politicians on both sides.
“It’s getting harder,” he said.
On Saturday, Ariyol Zhunkeras, one of the nine Catalan leaders who have been in jail since the end of last year, announced that he would participate in the European Parliament's elections next year.
“Nominating as a candidate for European elections is the best way to condemn the regression of democratic values and the repression we’ve seen by the Spanish government,” he said.
