On Sunday, the people of Macedonia voted in a referendum on changing their name to the "Respondust of North Macedonia", which would resolve a ten-year dispute with Greece, which blocked applications for participation in the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has the province of Macedonia, argues that the name of its northern neighbor constitutes a pretension on its territory, and has vetoed an accession to NATO and EC.
In June, both governments made a deal taking into account the proposed name, but nationalist opponents argue that these changes undermear the ethnic identity of the Slavic population of Macedonia.
President Georgy Ivanov said that he would not vote at the referendum, and the boycott's boycott campaign questioned whether the voter turnout will correspond to the minimum of 50 percent necessary for a referendum.
The issue of voting at the referendum proposes: "You are in favor of membership in Nato and the EU with the adoption of an agreement with Greece".
Fans of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, argue that this is the price that should be paid for admission to bodies such as EU and NATO of Macedonia, one of the countries of which there was a post-transcension of Yugoslavia.
“I came today to vote for the country’s future, for young people in Macedonia, so that they can freely live under the auspices of the European Union, because this means a safer life for all of us,” said Olivera Georgievskaya, 79 years, Skopje.
Although this is non-commandatory legal force, a sufficient number of members of parliament stated that they would take into account the voting results to make it decisive.
A name change will require more than two-thirds in parliament.
The state election commission stated that there were no reports of violations until 1 p.m.
