In front of the parliament building in Skopje Vladimir Kovardarkov, 54 years old, was preparing a small stage and laid out chairs in front of tents established by those who will boycott the referendum.
“We are for NATO and the EU, but we we want unification with raised heads, and not through service doors,” Kovadarkov said.
"We are a poor country, but we have an honor.
If they don't want to welcome us as Macedonia, we can appeal to others, such as China and Russia, and become a part of Eurasian integration."
The Prime Minister Zayev said that NATO's membership will bring the necessary investments in Macedonia, where the unemployment rate is more than 20 percent.
“I believe that the prevailing number will “pro” because more than ‒ 80% of our citizens are supported by the EC and NATO,” Zaev said after the vote.
He said that the "yes" will be "configuration of our future".
The poll, published last Monday by the Institute of Political Studies of Macedonia, said that the referendum will be attended by 30 to 43 percent of voters, which is below the necessary turnout turnout.
Another poll conducted by the Macedonian TV channel showed 57 percent of respondents planning to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70 percent said they would vote "yes".
For a successful referendum, the turnout should be 50 percent plus one vote.
Failure in the referendum will be the first serious strike on the policy of pro-Western government since its arrival last May.
