civil buildings under a blue flag.
In naval bases from Lake Titikaka to the Amazon, the motto "Our sea rightly" is outlined.
It is our duty to restore.
In the whole of Bolivia that has no access to the sea, the memory of the coastline lost by Chile in a bloody conflict due to the resources of the 19th century is still alive – as well as the desire to float on the Quiet Ocean again.
These hopes may have reached the maximum peak in recent decades as Bolivia is waiting for an international court's decision on October 1 after five years of discussion.
“Bolivia has an imputum, the spirit of unity and tranquility, and, of course, it expects a positive result,” Bolivian diplomat Rabert Calsadilla said.
Many Bolivians will be observing the decision of the CU on big screens across the country, hoping that the Tribunal in The Hague after decades of tense negotiations will support Bolivia's statement that Chile is obliged to negotiate to grant Bolivia a sovereign out-to-seam.
Evo Morales is a charismatic President of Bolivia who faces a controversial struggle for re-eligence next year.
“We’re very close to the return to the Pacific,”” he promised at the end of August.
But some analysts lears that the court is unlikely to make a decision in Bolivia's favor, and this will not change much, even if it does.
The U.N.-based organization in the Netherlands does not have the right to make a decision on the territory of Chile and said it would not determine the outcome of possible negotiations.
The fact that the decision of the International Court of the International Court to come out only six months after the final arguments were considered suggests that the case "was not complicated," said the Chilean expert in the field of international law Paz Zarate.
And without promoting Bolivia's affairs, the last four years, it may have been thrown back.
“The problem of access to the sea has been monopolized by the current Bolivian administration”, said Sarat.
According to here, Morales' militant rhetoric has lost the residual Chilean favor.
Bolivia and Chile will continue to talk, but after that it will be very difficult to hold discussions.
Since 1962, the two countries have not exchanged ambassadors.
Former Eduardio President Rodríguez Velté, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court's decision was unusually fast.
Monday will bring Bolivia "an exceptional opportunity to open a new era of relations with Chile" and an opportunity to "put the end of 139 years of disagreement with the mutual benefit," he said.
Kalsadilla also denied that Morales is still one of the most populous presidents of Latin America – used the maritime problem as a political support.
“Bolivia will never give up its right to access the Pacific,” he added.
“The ruling is an opportunity to see that we need to overcome the past.”
