John Edward: Knowledge of languages is a necessary skill for all citizens of the world
Independent Schools in Scotland have been retaining achievements in academic academic achievement, it has been going on since 2018 with another excellent result of exams, which is supported by individual and collective success in sports, art, music, and other community initiatives.
More than 30 000 pupils across Scotland's schools represented by the Scotch Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) seek to provide a best level of work for their students and parents.
Independent schools seek to prepare their students for further higher education, their chosen careers and their place as citizens of the world.
As an educational sector that can develop and implement an individual school curriculum, we see that modern languages continue to remain a popular and desirable subject in schools.
Nelson Mandela said, "If you're talking to a man in a language he understands, it stays in his head.
If you talk to him in his native language, it comes to his heart."
It's a vivid reminder that we can't rely only on English if we want to build relationships and trust people from other countries.
From the results of this year's recent exams, we see that languages occupy the first place in league tables with the highest scores in independent schools.
A total of 68 percent of students who studied foreign languages received the highest rating of A.
The data collected in 74 SCIS participating schools showed that 72 percent of pupils have reached a higher-ending A in the study of the Chinese language, while 72 percent – those who learn German, 69 percent – French, and 63 percent – Spanish, have also reached level A.
It shows that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as vital skills that will surely be useful for children and young people in the future.
Now languages as a subject are chosen in the same same as well as STEM (Science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the curriculum of independent schools and in other places.
A survey conducted by the UK Employmency and Skill Commission in 2014 found that among the reasons that employers cited due to difficulties when filling vacancies, 17 percent were due to a lack of language skills.
Therefore, more and more linguistic skills are becoming a necessity to prepare young people for a future career.
With more-perspective jobs that require language proficiencies, these skills become essential in a globalized world.
No relevant what kind of profession will choose, if you learn a different language, in the future it will become a real advantage if you have such skills for a lifetime.
The ability to communicate directly with people from other countries to automatically put a multilingual person in the first place among competitors.
According to a youGov survey, which was attended by more than 4,000 British adults in 2013, 75 percent failed to speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, and French was the only language in which two-digit percentages spoke – 15 percent.
That's why investing in language training is now important for today's kids.
Availability of several languages, especially in developing countries, will give children a better chance of finding a good job.
In Scotland, each school will be different in the languages it is taught.
A number of schools will focus on more classic modern languages, while others will teach languages that are considered the most important for the UK when looking at the future by 2020, such as Mandarian or japa.
No matter what your child's interest is, independent schools will always have a choice of several languages with a teaching syllable, which is experts in this field.
Scottish independent schools seek to provide a learning environment that will prepare children and equip them with the skills necessary to succeed, no matter what it expects in the future.
At present, it cannot be denied that in the global business environment the language is still vital for the country's future, so this should be reflected in education.
Indeed, modern languages should be treated as "intensive communication skills".
Independent schools will continue to offer this selection, diversity, and excellence for the youth of Scotland.
It's got to be done
John Eduard – Director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
