North Koroa says "in no way" will not disarrate unilaterally without creduality
North Korea's foreign minister said the United Nations continues sanctions that intensify distrust of the United States on Saturday and in such conditions the country cannot unilateralately abandon nuclear weapons.
Ry Yun Ho said at an annual General Assembly of the world body that last year North Korea has taken "significant measures of goodwill", such as an cessation of tests on nuclear and missile areas, the dismantling of a nuclear test zone, and a promise not to distribute nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“However we don’t see an appropriate response from the United States,” he said.
“There will be no confidence in the U.S. without confidence in our national saviour, and under such circumstances there′’s neithering to unilaterally disarm yourself.”
Despite the fact that Ri republished the usual North Korean complaints against Washington's resistance to a "stage" approach to dinuclearization, in which North Korea was rewarded as it took the gradual steps, his statement was significant that he did not denie unilateral declaration of deuclearization, which was done by Pyongyang in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement by Kim Jong-Un and Donald Trump in the first-ever U.S. president's summit and North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, when Kim promised to work on the way to "denuculerize the Korean Peninsula", Trump promised to provide security guarantees to North Korea.
North Korea seeks to formally end the war in Korea from 1950 to 53, but the United States said that Pyongyang must first abandon nuclear weapons.
Washington also resisted calling for the abolition of tough international sanctions against North Korea.
“The United States of America at first insist “denuclerization” and an increase in the level of pressure through sanctions in order to achieve their goal in a coercive way, and even oppose the ‘proclamation of the end of the war’,” says Ri.
"Perceiving the fact that the sanctions can put us on our knees is an impossible dream of people who do not know anything about us.
But the problem is that the continuing of sanctions deepens our distrust."
Ri did not mention plans for the second summit between Kim and Trump, which the U.S. leader noted earlier this week at the United Nations.
Instead, the minister drew attention to the three meetings between Kim and South Korea's leader, Moon Jain over the past five months and added: "If the participants in this issue of denuclearization were South Korea, and not the United States, the denuclerization of the Korean Peninsula would not become such a dead end."
However, the tone of Ri's speech differed sharply from last year when he spoke to the U.S. General Assembly that targeting a U.S. Continuing the U.S. with missiles using North Korean missiles, was unaviable after "Mr. Evil President" Trump called Kim a "smill"-suicide.
This year in the U.N., Trump, who threatened to "completely destroy" North Korea last year, praised Kim for his courage to reparate measures, but said there would still be a lot to do and the sanctions should remain in place as long as North Korea is denuculated.
On Wednesday, Trump said that he had no time frame for this, saying: "If it takes two years, three years or five months – it doesn't matter."
"China and Russia are arguing with the United Nations. The Security Council said it should reward Pyongyang for the measures taken.
Mike Pompeo told the UN Security Council on Thursday that “Fulfillment of security sanctions should be active and will definitely continue until we make sure the full, final and accurate dinucularization.”
The Security Council intensified sanctions against North Korea in 2006, trying to stop funding Pyongyang's nuclear and ballistic-missaction programs.
Pompeo met with Ri in Kaluaries of the Security Council. The General Assembly later stated that he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for the next summit.
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip was not successful.
In July, he left Pyongyang, saying the progress achieved with North Korea within a few hours of negotiations ended with a vis-à-à-üI'm claiming that he was “simplished by gangsters”.
At a meeting with Moon this month, North Korea promised to eliminate a rocket launch and a nuclear system if the United States takes "corresponding measures."
Trump said that in the words of Kim "compliant measures" that he awaits is a guarantee of security and a desire to normalize relations with Washington, which Trump promised in Singapore.
Harvard's students take a course on a proper vacation.
This year's Harvard University's new course should train students to sleep more and has a goal to deal with the culture to study at night with coffee in hand.
Scientist came to the conclusion that students at one of the best universities in the world often don't know how to properly care for themselves and for their health.
Charles Cheisler, Professor of Sleep Medicine at Harvard Medical Schedule and Brigham Hospital Specialist and Women's Hospital, has developed a course that he believes is the first of its kind in the United States.
Inspiriented by the performance on the effect of lack of sleep on training, he began the course.
At the end of the lecture, one girl came up to me and said: “Whine I was told this only now, in the last course of study?”
“She said that no one had ever told her about the importance of sleep – it surprised me,” he told The Telegraph.
For the first tim this year's course set forth to the students, explains why good sleep habits contribute to academic and sporting achievements, as well as improving the overall mood.
Paul Barreiro, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and Executive Director of the University's health service, said that the university decided to introduce a course after finding that students are often devoid of sleeping within a week.
The course that lasts an hour includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one section there is an image of a dormitory room, where students click on cups of coffee, curtains and books that talk about the effects of caffeine and light and how the lack of sleep affects athletic performance, as well as the importance of habits before bedtime.
At the next stage, the participants are told how perennial deprivation of sleep can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression, and cancer.
Campus map, with interactive drawings, encourages participants to think about their daily lives.
“We know that this will not instantly change students’ behavior.”
But we believe that they should know this in the same same as everyone knows about the consequences of smoking for health," Professor Chaisler added.
According to him, the culture of night gatherings continues to exist, adding that modern technologies and the ever-increasing pressure on students leads to deprivation of slot, which is an increasing problem.
“Preciminated sufficient good quality sleep should “secret weapon” of students to deal with stress, depletion and anxiety,” he said, “even to avoid excess weight, since the deprivation of sleep makes the brain constantly hungry.”
Raymond Sa, a 19-year-old California student who studies chemical and physical biology, has assisted Professor Chaisler in developing a course.
He said the course opened his eyes and was inspired to train everyone on campus.
He hopes that the next step will be the request for all orients to complete a similar training program before entering the university.
Professor Chaisler recommended that students have had the opportunity to set the clock to know when to go to bed and when to wake up, being aware of the harmful effects of "blue light" emited from monitor screens and LED illuminators, which can change the daily mode, which leads to backfilling problems.
