HIV/AIDS: China reports an increase in new cases by 14%
China announced that the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS has increased by 14%.
According to health care workers, more than 820 000 people were infected in the country.
Only in the second quarter of 2018, about 40 000 new cases were registered.
The vast majority of new cases have been transmitted through sex, as it was in the past.
Traditionally, HIV infection spreads in some parts of China as a result of infection due to blood transfusions.
"However, the number of such people infected with HIV due to blood translusions has decreased almost to zero," Chinese officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
Every year, however, the number of those who live with HIV and AIDS in China grows by 100 000 people.
The transmission of HIV infection through sex is an acute problem of the LGBT community in China.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but there is still discrimination against LGBT people.
Due to the country's credulative values in the country approximately 70-90% of men who have sex with men end up marrying women.
Many diseases spread from inadequate knowledge and sexual contraception.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to drugs against HIV as part of efforts to address this global issue.
