What is an international festival of citizens and what has been done to reduce poverty?
An international festival takes place this Saturday in New York: an annual music event that has a very impressive composition of speakers and performers and has a mission to end poverty in the world.
Now, for seven years, the Global Citizenship Festival will take place on the big lawn of the Central Park, where tens of thousands of people will come to enjoy the Janet Jackson Concert Concert, Cardy B and Sean Mendes, as well as to learn the real goal of the event — cessation of poverty and poverty by 2030.
The International Citizenship Festival, which appeared in 2012, is a continuation of the Global project against the poverty of an international advocacy group that hopes to end poverty through an increase in the number of people who are actively struggling against it.
In order to receive a free ticket for the event (if you don't want to paid a VIP ticket), visitors had to perform a number of tasks or "hold an action", to be a volunteer, to write an e-mail to the leader of the country, to call any other figures and to raise awareness about the purpose of cessation of poverty.
But how successful was the Civic festival for 12 years and was it able to achieve its goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with an unprecedented concert in a real way to convince people of the need for active action, or is it just a different case of so-called "kittwism" – people feel that they're doing a good job by signing an online petition or sending a tweet?
Since 2011, the organizers of the festival have stated that they have recorded more than 19 million "actions" from their admirers who seek to achieve various goals.
