The inventor of the global network plans to start the new Internet to compete with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, an inventor of the global network, launches a start-up that will become a competitor to Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest technology legend project, Inrupt, is a company that has been built on the Berners-Lee Solid open platform.
Solid allows users to select the storage location and who has permission to access this information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company Berners-Lee made a joke that, creating Inrupt, he meant "dominance over the world".
“We need to do it now,” he said about the start-up.
"This is a historic moment."
The program uses Solid technology to allow people to create "ower online data repositories" or POD.
It can contain contact listings, to-do lists, calendar, fanatics, and other personal and professional tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are available all at once in one browser at the same time.
The uniqueness of the personal data warehouse on the Internet is that only the user specifies who has access to what type of information.
The company calls it a "personal capacity-increasing through data."
According to the Executive Director of the company John Bruce, the idea of creating Inrupt gives the company an opportunity to bring together the resources, process, process and the necessary skills to make Solid available to everyone.
The company now consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security-play platform bought by IBM, several developers working on a contract's contract project and volunteer coders.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world can create their own decentralized programs using tools available on the Inrupt website.
Bruners-Lee stated that he and his team didn’t spoken to “Facebook and Gougho on whether to introduce or non-fulfilled changes if all of their business models are instantly revised.”
“We don’t ask their permission.”
On Saturday's publication on Medium Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt's mission "is providing commercial energy and the ecosystem to protect the integrity and quality of the new web created on Solid."
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet, creating the Consortium of the World Wide Web at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has had a very influential advocate for the neutrality of the network.
Even when launching Inrupt, Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the Consortium of the World Network, the Network Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
“I’m very optimistic about the new era of the network,” Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Wang: Honoring the Priest awarded the Victoria Cross in the First World War
The only priest of the Anglican Church, awardeed by the Victoria Cross during the First World War as a participant in the fighting, was marked in his city 100 years later.
Lieutenant Colonel Bernard Wang was awardeed September 29, 1918 for taking part in an attack near Belengliz and Leacour.
However, four days later, he died from a sniper bullet and never found out that he had received the highest military british award.
On Saturday in the town of Rashden, the county of Narthamptanshir, two of his grandchildren opened a commemorative stone at the parade.
One of his grandchildren, Michael Wang, said it was "very symbolic" that the stone opens up exactly 100 years after his grandfather was rewarded.
According to London Gazette, on September 29, 1918 Lieutenant Colonel Van led his battalion through the canal de San Quentine "a thick mist and under a strong gun and machine-gun fire of the enemy."
Then he ran into the line of fire and "with great couragence" led the soldiers forward, captivating the cannon on his own and knocking out three units.
Lieutenant colonel was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, and a little more than a month the war ended.
72-year-old Michael Wang said that his grandfather's actions were "something that I would never have been able to don't do and also something worthy of respect."
HÜmüh and his brother Dr. James Wang also laid a wreath after the parade, which was headed by the Bruntwood Imperial Youth Orchestra.
Michael Wang said that "it's a great honor for him to take part in the parade" and added that “the courage of a true hero has been demonstrated by the support provided by many.”.”
