Descedue of the new German warship again postponed
Germany’s new friggate was supposed to enter into operation in 2014 to replace the old warries of the Cold War, but it will appear no earlier than the next year due to faulty systems and the ever-increasing prices, according to local media reports.
Descendation Rheinland-Pfalz, the main splorship of a completely new-class frigates Baden-Wuerttemberg, was postponed until the first half of 2019, according to the newspaper Die Zeit, citing a military representative.
The ship was supposed to enter into service in 2014, but the problems that emerged after construction prevented this ambitious project.
Four warships class Baden-Wuerttemberg, commissioned back in 2007, must replace the old frigates of Bremen class.
They will be armed with strong guns, various anti-ship and anti-aircraft missiles, as well as some stealth technologies, for example, reduced visibility for radars, infrared and acoustic signatures.
Among other important characteristics – longer periods between maintenance — newer frigates may not enter their ports for up to two years.
However, permanent delays mean that modern warships – which can allow Germany to demonstrate its tire outside the country – will become obsolete as soon as they finally come into operation.
Unhappied frigate F125 hit the headlines last year when the German Navy officially refused to take the switch and returned its Blohm & Voss shipyard to Hamburg.
For the first time the naval navy returned the sboard to the shipyard after delivery.
Little is known about the reasons for the return, but the German media wrote about numerous "deffects in software and equipment", which make the shirl in vain in the combat mission.
Disadvantages of the software were particularly important, since Baden-Wuerttemberg class ships have only 120 sailors – half of the crew of the old Bremen class frigates.
It also turned out that the swill has a lot of weight, which reduces its performance and limits the possibility of further updates.
"Rheinland-Pfalz" weighs 7000 tons, which is twice the weight of ships of a similar class used by Germany in World War II.
In addition to the defective equipment, the price of the whole project — including crew training – is also becoming a problem.
The price has reached an incredible €3.1 billion (€3.6 billion), although at first it was €2.2 billion.
Problems accumulated with new frigates are becoming particularly important in light of recent warnings that Germany's maritime power is decreasing.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, the head of the committee for defense in the German parliament, said the Navy "has fewer and fewer ships capable for military missions".
He said that the problem increases with time, because the old ships are decommissioned, and there are no ships to be replaced.
He complained that none of the baden-Wuerttemberg class frigates are not able to join the Navy.
The National Society for the Protection of Monuments listened to the secret life of bats
A new study, which is carried out in the Scottish Highlands, aims to detect how bats use the landscape in the hunt for food.
Scientists are hoping to find something new in the behavior of unique flying mammals and help in future actions to preserve these animals.
The study of the scientists of the National Society for the Conservation of Monuments of Scotland is engaged in small bats and also brown long-eared bats of Dabanton in Inverya gardens in Western Ross.
The special recording tools will be placed in the main locations of this area to keep an eye out during the season.
Employees of the Society and Volunteers will carry out mobile studies using manual detectors.
An expert analysis of the sounds of all recordings to determine the frequency of calls of bats, as well as which bats are doing.
Map of residence and report will be used to create a detailed picture of the behavior of these animals in this landscape.
Rob Düar, adviser to the National Society for Conservation of Nativity hopes that the results will show which areas are most important to the bats and how they are used by each of the species.
This information will help to determine the benefits of creating habitats like meadows, and how to maintain forests to live bats and other associated animals.
The number of bats in Scotland and the United Kingdom has decreased significantly over the past century.
They are threatened by construction and development work that affect the place of residence and destroy them.
Wind turbines and lighting can be a risk, as well as sticky tape for flies and some chemicals for building materials. They are also threatened by domestic cats.
Bats aren't at all blind.
However, because of their nightlife, the ears of bats are more sensitive than the eyes when it comes to hunting.
They use a sophisticated echolate technique to detect beetles and obstacles on the path of flight.
The national society, which is responsible for more than 270 buildings, 38 important gardens and 76 000 hectares of land throughout the country, takes bats very seriously seriously.
It employs ten trained experts who regularly carry out researches, checks of habitats and, sometimes rescue activities.
The organization even created the first and onle Scotch bat reserve in the Trile estate in Dumphris and Halowai, where eight of the ten species of bats of Scotland live.
The head of the estate, David Thompson says it's an ideal territory for bats.
“Here in Trive, we just have a great place for bats,” he said.
“We have old buildings, a lot of old trees and a very good place of residence.
But we still don’t know a lot, so our work here and in other estates will help to understand what they need for a better life.”
It emphasizes the importance of checking the presence of bats before carrying out the repair of property, as it is unintentional to destroy one sedal, killing up to 400 females and young bats, which will lead to the complete extermination of the local population.
Bats are protected and they are forbidden to kill, fright, disturb or destroy their saddles.
Elizabet Ferral, an employee of the Bat Protection Organization in Scotland, urges the public to help in the matter.
She says: “We still have a lot to learn about our bats, and we don’t even know how their populations live.”
