According to the new plan of the conservatives, foreign buyers of British real estate will pay a higher fee, and additional money will be used to assist the homeless
This action neutralizes Korbin’s success initiative to attract young voters’
Raising state duty will abolish those who do not pay taxes in the UK
The Ministry of Financy plans to receive up to £12 million a year to help homeless
Theresa May will now announce that foreign buyers of British real estate will pay a higher state fee, and additional money will be used to help the homeless.
This action is seen as an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbin's success initiative to attract young voters as well as giving affordable housing. It is aimed at high-income people.
Raising state duty is aimed at persons and firms that do not pay taxes in the UK, and the money will go to the government program of giving housing to the homeless.
Raising the current state duty, including higher levels adopted two years ago, on the purchase of second houses and housing for employment, can reach up to three percent.
The Ministry of Finance hopes to receive up to £12 million a year.
Approximately 13 percent of new buildings in London are bought by UK non-residents, this leads to price increases and new customers find it difficult to buy their first housing.
Many rich regions of the country – especially the capital — became “ghost cities”, because a large number of buyers from abroad do not live most of the time in the UK.
The new policy appeared just a few weeks after Boris Johnson’s call to reduce the state duty for young buyers in order to facilitate the acquisition of first housing.
He blamed large construction firms for keeping high real estate prices by seizing the land and non-use of it, and urged Ms. May to abolish quotas for affordable housing to send "shammomile situation with housing" in Britain.
Mr. Corbin has announced a voice series of housing reforms, including rent control and the cessation of "unreasonable" eviction.
Also, he wants to transfer more power to the local councils in the field of housing construction.
May said: "Last year, I stated that I would dedicate my work as prime minister to the restoration of a British dream – life for each new generation should become a better.
This means the improvement of our imperfect housing market.
Britain will always be open to those who want to live here, work and build their lives.
However, it's unreasumed that people who don't residing in the UK and foreign companies can buy real estate as easily as British workers.
For too many people, the dream of having their own home is becoming more unattainable, and the problem of the homeless remains very real."
