According to the study is the perfect time to drink a pintu beer – 17:30 on Saturday
The hot summer has increased the profits of British pubs fighting for survival, but brought pressure on the chain of restaurants.
According to statistics of the group of pubs and bars noted a sales increase by 2.7 percent in July, but in restaurants sales decreased by 4.8 percent.
Peter Martin from the CGA's consulting business group that collected this statist, said: “Longing sunny weather and longer than expected, the participation of the English national team in the final of the World Cup, means that in Junury it happened that in June, when bushes increased sales by 2.8 percent, but the restaurants suffered even more.
Reducing sales in restaurants by 1.8 percent in June, only aggravated in July.
Pubs and bars, which mainly sell beverages, have shown the best result in contrast to restaurants.
The pub, which sells more food, also baked in the sun, although not as much as restaurants.
People just wanted to drink.
The sale of drinks has increased by 6.6% in pubs and food sales have decreased by three percent."
Paul Nyuman, an RSM analyst on recreation and accommodation said: "This results have become a trend that we've been seeing since late April.
The weather and the influence of important social and sporting events remain the most important factors when it comes about the volume of sales in the catering market.
It's not surprising that restaurants continue to fight, because sales have decreased by 4.8 percent per year, and it's very unpleasant when prices are constantly rising.
The long and hot summer came at the most bad time for operators who are focused on food, and time will tell whether the lower temperatures in August will bring such an expected relief."
The total increase in pubs and restaurants, including newly opened, amounted to 2.7 percent in July, reflecting a slowdown in brand-deployment.
Tool tracking sales in British pubs, bars and restaurants Coffer Peach Tracker collects and analyses performance data from £47 operating groups with a total turnover of £9 billion, and is an important benchmark in the industry.
