Earthquake and tsunami in Indonesia
According to officials, at least 384 people were killed in a powerful earthquake and tsunami, which collapsed in the Indonesian city of Palou on Friday and expected to rise.
Due to a communication shut-off, humanitarian aid officials have not been able to receive any information from Dongala's regency, an area north of Palu, which is closer to the epicenter of an earthquake of 7.5 points.
More than 16 000 people were evacuated after a natural disaster.
Here are some key facts about Palu and Dongalo on the island of Sulawes:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulawesi province, located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of the island of Sulawesi, with an estimated population of 379 800 in 2017.
When an earthquake and a tsunami came, the city celebrated the 40th anniversary of its founding.
Dongala is a regency that extends along more than 180 miles (300 km) coastline in the north-west island of Sulawesi.
The Regency is an administrative unit as besid lower than the provincium, there were 299 200 people in 2017.
Fisheries and agriculture are the basis of the economy of Central Sulawesi province, especially the coastal region of Dongalo.
Nickel's mining is also essential for the province, but is mainly concentrated in the Moravians on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
According to the Indonesian Disastribulant Misconception Agency, Palu and Dongalo have been subjected several times over the past 100 years to a tsunami.
In 1938, more than 200 people were killed from the tsunami in Dongala, and hundreds of houses were destroyed.
The tsunami also struck the western part of Dongall in 1996, resulting in killing nine people.
Indonesia is located in a seismic-active Pacific ring of fiery and it regularly shakes earthquakes.
Below is a list of major earthquakes and tsunami in recent years:
2004: A strong earthquake on the west coast of the Indonesian province of Aceh in northern Sumatra caused a tsunami that hit 14 countries, killing 226 000 people along the coast of the Indian Ocean, more than than half of them in Achah.
2005: A series of strong earthquakes crashed on the west coast of Sumatra in late March-early April.
Hundreds of people have died on the island of Niyas, off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: An earthquake of magnitude 6.8 occurred in the south of Java, the most densely populous island of Indonesia, causing a tsunami that crashed on the southern coast, killing about 700 people.
In 2009, in the city of Padang, the capital of the province of Western Sumatra, there was an earthquake of magnitude 7.6.
More than 1100 people were killed.
2010: An 7.5-point earthquake occurred on one of the Mentova Islands outside Sumatra, causing a tsunami to a height of up to 10 meters that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A weapon earthquake struck Pidi Jaya's regency in the province of Acheh, causing destruction and panick, as it recalled the devastation as a result of the deprivation of the 2004 deadly earthquake and tsunami.
This tsunami didn't came, but more than 100 people were killed under the ruins.
2019: Serious earthquakes hit Indonesia's tourist island, killing more than 500 people, mainly in the north of the island.
An earthquake destroyed thousands of buildings and left thousands of tourists without the opportunity to leave.
