The federal prosecutor's office demands a rare death sentence for a suspected terrorist attack in New York
The Federal Prosecutor's Officipice in New York seeks the death penalty for Saifula Saipova, a suspected terrorist attack in New York, which killed eight people – a rare penalty that has not been applied in the state for federal crimes since 1953.
Saipov, 30 years old, in October, allegedly rented the company "Home Depot" to hit a bicycle track along the West Side Chasé in Lower Manhattan, beating pedestrians and cyclists on his way.
To justify the death sentence, according to the notification of the intention to seek the death penalty filed in the Southern District of New York, prosecutors must prove that Saipov "intentively" killed eight people and "intentially" inflicted serious bodily injuries.
According to the materials of the court, for both offenses there is a possible punishment in the form of the death penalty.
A few weeks after the attack, a large federal investigative juvenile Saipova had filed charges from 22 items that included eight accusations of murder to support the racket; this item is commonly used by federal prosecutors for organized crime, as well as a charge of violence and extremination of vehicles.
According to the prosecutors, the attack demanded "diligent and deliberate planning", and describating Saipov's crimes committed, used such phrases as "disgustable, cruel and deprivated".
“Sifula Khabibulaevich Saipov caused an injury, inflicted the damage and the losses to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicolas Kleves, Ana-Lora Decadet, Darena Drake, Ariel Erlius, Hernano Feruci, Hernana Diego Mendas and Alejandro Domiána Pagnuk,”
Five of the dead were tourists from Argentina.
Ten years have passed since the last criminal case against the death penalty in the Southern District of New York.
Khalid Barns was convicted of murdering two drug suppliers, but was eventually sentenced to life in prison in September 2009.
For the last time, the death penalty in New York was applied for a federal case in 1953 to Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married couple executed after they had been sentencedened two years earlier for espionage in favor of the Soviet Union during the Cold War.
Rosenberg was conquered in an electric chair on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, a native of Uzbekistan, according to judicial documents, demonstrated the absence of reproaches of conscience in the days and months after the attack.
According to the police, he told the investigators that he felt the pleasure of his act.
According to the indictment, Saipov told the authorities that he was inspired to watch the Islamic State's video on the phone.
He also asked for permission to hang the Islamic State's flag in the hospital ward, the police said.
He pleads not guilty in the indictment, which consists of 22 points.
David Patan, one of the federal public defenders representing Saipova, said they were “unconditionally disappointed” by the prosecutor’s decision.”
“We believe that the decision to apply the death penalty, rather than life imprisonment without the possibility of exemption, only prolong the pain from these events for all participants,” Paton said.
Saipov's defense team had previously asked prosecutors not to seek the death penalty.
