Why the trade war between China and the United States could be good for Beijing
The first volleys of trade war between China and the United States were deafening, and since the war is far away from the end, misunderstandings between the countries may be in favor of Beijing, experts say.
U.S. President Donald Trump shot the first warning earlier this Year by taxing China’s main export goods, including solar panels, steel and aluminium.
The most significant escalation took place this week when the new tariffs affected goods for a total of €200 billion (150 billion pounds), effectively taxing half of the goods falling to the United States from China.
Each time, Beijing responded in the same way, raising tariffs from five to ten percent on U.S. goods worth $60 billion.
"China has pledged a symmetrical response to the United States, and the second-last economy is unlikely to lead an eye."
To force Washington to retreat, you need to delve deeper into the demands, but a public incidence to the United States is almost unacceptable for the Chinese leader Xi Jinping.
However, experts say that if Beijing uses the situation correctly, the U.S. trade war in the long term can support China, reducing the mutual dependence of these two countries.
“The fact that a quick political decision in Washington or Beijing could create conditions that will begin the process of a sharp economic downfall is much more unsafe than the observers believed earlier,” said Abigale Greys, an auxiliary researcher specializing in Asia, in the research and analytical center of the new American security.
Syria "ready" to the return of refugees, says Foreign Minister
Syria says it's ready for the return of refugees and calls for assistance in the reconstruction of a country destroyed by a war that lasted more than seven years.
Addressing the UN General Assembly, Foreign Minister Valid al-Mualle said that the conditions in the country are improving.
“As of today, the situation in the country is more stable and safe, thanks to progress in combating terrorism,” he said.
The government continues to restore areas destroyed by terrorists to return to normal.
Now we have all the conditions for the return of refugees to a country they had to leave because of terrorism and targeted economic measures that were aimed at improving their daily life and means of life.
The United Nations believes that more than 5.5 million Syrians have left the country since the beginning of the war in 2011.
Another six millions living in the country require humanitarian aid.
Al-Muallem said that the Syrian regime will gladly accept help to rebuild a destroyed country.
However, he stressed that the country will not accept assistance in exchange for certain conditions or assistance from countries sponsoring rebels.
